  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 340 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heavy rainfall and strong wind gusts are possible \nthis afternoon and evening, mainly near and south of the VA/NC \nborder.\n\nThe surface front remains stalled along the VA/NC border this \nafternoon. Showers and storms have largely been confined south of \nthe boundary where instability is maximized. The best shear is noted \nto the north of the front but enough extends southward into NC to \nallow for a few instances of transient rotation in the storms so far \ntoday. However, the main threat from convection today will continue \nto favor strong winds and locally heavy rainfall. SPC has trimmed \nthe Marginal Risk for severe storms to areas roughly along and S of \nthe US-58 corridor. CAM guidance has been pretty abysmal over the \nlast few days but several models suggest the potential for \nadditional showers/storms into central VA this evening into the \novernight as the stalled front finally lifts back to the north. Warm \nand muggy conditions expected tonight with lows in the low/mid 70s \nand increase dew points behind the frontal passage. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for strong to severe storms and flooding \nwill return area-wide on Tuesday and Wednesday ahead of a cold front.\n\nSevere potential ramps up considerably on Tuesday as an upper trough \namplifies over the Great Lakes. Several shortwaves will traverse the \narea ahead of the main trough axis. Flow aloft strengthens \nconsiderably by the afternoon hours with 40-50 kt of flow noted at \n500mb. Low level moisture also ramps up with PWAT values climbing \nback above 2\". Instability is a bit more uncertain with clouds \npossibly hampering heating to some extent but models still show 2000-\n3000 J/kg of MLCAPE across the area. Storm mode is also somewhat \nuncertain with the potential for a mix of linear segments and semi-\ndiscrete cells. All these factors point to a robust severe wx threat \nacross the region. SPC has most of the area in a Slight Risk (level \n2/5) with a sliver of Enhanced Risk (lvl 3/5) clipping far northern \nportions of the MD Eastern Shore. Could see the higher risk area \nbeing shifted a bit south and east in subsequent outlooks pending \ngreater clarity on degree of cloud cover and storm morphology. As is \ntypical for Mid-Atlantic July severe setups, the main threat will be \nfrom damaging winds but a tornado or two cannot be completely ruled \nout, especially across the N and NE portions of the region where the \nshear and low level helicity are progged to be strongest. \n\nThe severe threat likely continues into Wednesday, but with the \nhigher probabilities shifted to the south and east ahead of the cold \nfront. How convection evolves on Tuesday will also influence the \ndegree of cloud cover and destabilization potential on Wednesday. \nThat said, the flow aloft is forecast to remain rather strong. SPC \nhas a Slight Risk outlined from Richmond south and east with a \nMarginal Risk (lvl 1/5) for the remainder of the area. \n\nDespite the ongoing drought conditions, Tuesday and Wednesday both \nhave the potential for localized flooding, especially in urban and \npoor drainage areas. WPC has most of the area in a Slight Risk ERO \non Tuesday and Wednesday with PWAT generally 2-2.25\". Decent flow \naloft should keep individual cells moving but cell training could \nresult in areas of flash flooding. Some drier air finally moves back \ninto the region Thursday and Friday but some showers and storms will \nremain possible, mainly across the southern and southeastern \nportions of the area.",
